WHPX keeps a real dirty log. Its log_sync marked the whole section dirty
at every call, so a display device under WHPX found its whole scanout
changed at every refresh and redrew, copied and uploaded all of it: on a
Windows host the d3dpt-vga desktop of Windows 11 grew choppier with the
mode (2560x1440 already, 3840x2160 worse), where KVM and HVF, which
track pages, stay smooth. A RAM section with a dirty log (VGA's VRAM) is
now mapped with WHvMapGpaRangeFlagTrackDirtyPages, log_start / log_stop
map it again with or without the flag, and log_sync takes the pages the
guest wrote from WHvQueryGpaRangeDirtyBitmap (which clears them) into
QEMU's bitmap. A hypervisor without the call, or an unaligned section,
keeps the old whole-section answer; WHPX_DIRTY_TRACK=0 is the A/B.
Applies after patch 89, the other whpx-common.c one.
Drop when upstream WHPX tracks dirty pages.

diff --git a/accel/whpx/whpx-common.c b/accel/whpx/whpx-common.c
index e75efac..a6066b9 100644
--- a/accel/whpx/whpx-common.c
+++ b/accel/whpx/whpx-common.c
@@ -14,6 +14,7 @@
